# desktop-packages.list - every Debian package the amd64 desktop asks for by name.
#
# The desktop root is a Debian bookworm base with our userland laid over
# it (tools/mkdesktop.sh says why). For a long time the answer to "what
# is in that base" lived in two throwaway scripts at the top of the base
# directory itself, outside the repository, and the only complete record
# was `apt-mark showmanual` on the one machine that had built it. This
# file is that record, in the repository, and tools/apply-packages.sh is
# the only thing that installs from it.
#
# One package per line; `#` starts a comment. Only the packages somebody
# asked for are here, not what they pull in - apt resolves the rest, and
# a list of 900 names nobody chose is a list nobody can review. The
# packages debootstrap itself put down (coreutils, dpkg, libc6 ...) are
# not listed either: they are the base, not a choice.
#
# Installed with --no-install-recommends. A Recommends in Debian is a
# guess about what most people want next, and on this image each guess
# is disk and download that nobody reviewed.

# ── the session (shell-design-spec.md 8) ─────────────────────────────
sway
swayidle
swaylock
wayfire
xwayland
waybar
fuzzel
foot
# The terminal (kgx): GTK 4, the same title bar as every other window.
gnome-console
mako-notifier
wl-clipboard
wtype
grim
slurp
wlr-randr
wlsunset
playerctl
brightnessctl
libnotify-bin
dbus-x11
xdg-desktop-portal
xdg-desktop-portal-gtk
xdg-desktop-portal-wlr
# Flatpak, for the applications Debian does not package (Discord,
# Spotify, Zoom, Chrome, VS Code, Steam ...) and newer versions of the
# ones it does: the Software app installs them from Flathub through
# lp-privd. bubblewrap, which it pulls in, is its sandbox; that needs
# the kernel's user namespaces, and the document portal needs FUSE
# (kernel/lp-zero-amd64.fragment).
flatpak
xdg-utils
desktop-file-utils
shared-mime-info
gnome-themes-extra
papirus-icon-theme
xkb-data

# ── the platform under it: devices, seat, bus, disks, sound ──────────
udev
seatd
dbus
policykit-1
udisks2
gvfs
gvfs-backends
gvfs-fuse
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
wireplumber
pavucontrol
alsa-utils
upower
# Bluetooth: bluetoothd and bluetoothctl. The XPS's QCA6174/BCM4350
# combo cards carry a Bluetooth controller on USB; without bluez it is
# there and nothing can talk to it. bluetoothd is started from
# /etc/services, not by this package (there is no systemd to start it).
bluez

# ── applications (apps-and-settings.md 3-1) ─────────────────────────
# gedit and eog stand in for what bookworm does not have yet
# (gnome-text-editor is here too; loupe is not packaged for bookworm).
firefox-esr
gedit
gnome-text-editor
eog
evince
gnome-calculator
gnome-clocks
file-roller
mpv
gthumb
drawing
baobab
gnome-disk-utility
gnome-font-viewer
ffmpeg
gstreamer1.0-libav
gstreamer1.0-plugins-good
gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad
# The owner's list of everyday apps (the other OS's screenshot): an
# office suite - documents, spreadsheets, slides - drawn with GTK so it
# looks like everything else, in Korean too; a code editor; a partition
# manager beside GNOME Disks; a system monitor; a window for mpv.
libreoffice-writer
libreoffice-calc
libreoffice-impress
libreoffice-gtk3
libreoffice-l10n-ko
geany
gparted
gnome-system-monitor
celluloid
# Korean from a real keyboard: fcitx5 with the Hangul engine (두벌식 by
# default). The on-screen keyboard has its own Hangul composer; this is
# for the XPS's keyboard. Started by lp-shell-start when installed.
fcitx5
fcitx5-hangul
fcitx5-frontend-gtk3
fcitx5-frontend-gtk4
fcitx5-config-qt
# Qt's Wayland platform: without it every Qt program (the input method's
# settings above among them) stopped at "could not find the Qt platform
# plugin wayland" and never opened a window.
qtwayland5

# ── type ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
fonts-dejavu-core
fonts-nanum
fonts-nanum-coding
fonts-noto-color-emoji
# Hangul in the interface. Nanum covers Korean text in documents, but
# the UI font stack falls back through Noto, and without the CJK set a
# Korean label in a GTK dialog can come out as boxes. Checked with
# `fc-list :lang=ko`.
fonts-noto-cjk

# ── language and time ────────────────────────────────────────────────
locales
tzdata

# ── the port table ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /etc/services is glibc's getservbyname() table, and on a Debian
# system netbase owns it (with /etc/protocols beside it). Our init's
# list of supervised programs used to sit at that path; it now lives at
# /etc/lp/services (init and `service` read it there first), so the
# name can go back to meaning what every other program expects.
netbase

# ── what a person expects to find at a prompt (the owner's list) ─────
# apt talks to deb.debian.org over https; without ca-certificates the
# machine can verify nothing it downloads and `apt update` fails on the
# first boot. It was missing from the base until this list existed.
ca-certificates
openssh-client
curl
rsync
git
vim
nano
tmux
screen
htop
tree
ncdu
file
plocate
zip
unzip
man-db
at
python3-pip
bsdextrautils

# ── network ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
iproute2
dnsutils
traceroute
iputils-tracepath
iputils-ping
iw
rfkill
# WPA3. LP's own daemon (userland/wpa) does WPA2-PSK's handshake; a
# network that takes only WPA3 (SAE) or requires protected management
# frames is handed, for that one connection, to Debian's wpa_supplicant,
# which does both (wpa.c, "WPA3"). Around Korean homes most networks are
# WPA3 now - phones' hotspots, the ISPs' routers.
wpasupplicant

# ── hardware and power ───────────────────────────────────────────────
pciutils
usbutils
kmod
lm-sensors
# Intel's thermal daemon: keeps a Skylake laptop (the XPS 9550's
# i7-6700HQ) under its limits with RAPL and powerclamp before the BIOS's
# own emergency throttling does it harder - Ubuntu runs it on the same
# machine. lp-tune does what power-profiles-daemon would (the profiles);
# this is the part it does not. Started by init (tools/mkdesktop.sh).
thermald
acpi
powertop

# ── firmware for every other machine ─────────────────────────────────
# What the modules of kernel/lp-desktop-amd64.fragment load from
# /lib/firmware: Radeon and Intel graphics (i915's also goes into the
# kernel's initramfs, tools/mkdisk.sh), NVIDIA for nouveau, the Wi-Fi and
# Bluetooth of Intel, Realtek, MediaTek, Qualcomm/Atheros, Broadcom and
# Marvell, the laptop speaker amplifiers (Cirrus), and Intel's sound DSP.
# From bookworm-backports (tools/apply-packages.sh pins firmware-* there).
# Left out: server NICs, Qualcomm phone SoCs, capture cards.
firmware-amd-graphics
firmware-intel-graphics
firmware-nvidia-graphics
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-realtek
firmware-mediatek
firmware-atheros
firmware-brcm80211
firmware-libertas
firmware-cirrus
firmware-intel-sound
firmware-intel-misc
firmware-misc-nonfree
firmware-sof-signed

# ── disks: what the installer runs, and what a person mounts ─────────
# lp-install partitions with sfdisk (fdisk), makes the EFI partition
# with mkfs.vfat (dosfstools) and copies the root with rsync. efibootmgr
# is not used by the installer - it writes the boot entry itself - but
# it is how a person reads and repairs one.
fdisk
gdisk
parted
e2fsprogs
dosfstools
exfatprogs
ntfs-3g
efibootmgr
# The disks track's partition manager (lp-diskd) and the recovery
# partition's tools: resizing FAT, btrfs, LUKS2, disk health, and the
# compressor the recovery's reinstall payload is packed with.
fatresize
btrfs-progs
cryptsetup
smartmontools
nvme-cli
zstd

# ── graphics (the fonts-and-graphics track's list) ───────────────────
# Mesa's GL (iris) is already here as a dependency of the desktop. These
# add Vulkan, hardware video decode for the HD 530 - intel-media's iHD
# driver, from non-free, because Skylake's HEVC and VP9 decode is only
# in that build - and the two tools that say whether either works.
mesa-vulkan-drivers
intel-media-va-driver-non-free
vainfo
mesa-utils

# ── building on the machine ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The desktop programs link against this base's GTK 4.8, so they are
# built in it. Keeping the compiler on the image costs about 200MB and
# makes the machine able to rebuild its own applications.
gcc
make
pkg-config
libc6-dev
libgtk-4-dev
libgtk-3-dev
libgtk-layer-shell-dev
python3-gi
gir1.2-gtk-3.0
